\begin{align*}
\sum_{n = 1}^\infty \frac{1}{n(n + 2)} &= \sum_{n = 1}^\infty \left(\frac{1}{n} - \frac{1}{n + 2}\right) \\
&= \left(\frac{1}{1} - \frac{1}{3}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{4}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{3} - \frac{1}{5}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{6}\right) + \dotsb \\
&= \left(\frac{1}{1} + \frac{1}{2}\right) - \left(\frac{1}{3} + \frac{1}{4}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{5} + \frac{1}{6}\right) - \left(\frac{1}{7} + \frac{1}{8}\right) + \dotsb.
\end{align*}
We can group the terms in pairs and see that each pair sums to $\frac{3}{2} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{3}{4}$.
So the sum of the infinite series is
\[\left(\frac{3}{4} + \frac{3}{4} + \frac{3}{4} + \dotsb\right) = \boxed{\frac{3}{4}}.\]
